# mac-plus - a Macintosh Plus (1986): the map, the VIA, the video and the SCC.
#
#   rsemu run mac-plus --media macrom=Mac-Plus.ROM
#   rsemu run mac-plus -p ram=4M --media macrom=Mac-Plus.ROM
#   rsemu run mac-plus --media macrom=Mac-Plus.ROM --vnc :5900
#
# With no disk the ROM chimes, sizes memory, runs its memory test and settles
# on the Macintosh's grey desktop with the arrow cursor in the top left corner,
# with its 60.15 Hz tick chain running. It does **not** reach the insert-disk
# screen yet; `docs/platforms/mac-plus.md` has the ledger of what is missing.
# The picture reaches a person through `--vnc`, which serves the video
# circuit's scanout.
#
# Written from *Guide to the Macintosh Family Hardware* (Apple Computer, 2nd
# edition) - chapter 3 for the address map and the overlay, the VIA chapter
# for the port assignments, the video chapter for the raster - and from the
# Synertek SY6522 and Zilog Z8530 data sheets. **No Macintosh emulator source
# was consulted and the ROM was not disassembled**; where the documents were
# ambiguous the answer came from watching which addresses a real ROM touches
# (ROADMAP.md 1, CLAUDE.md). No byte of any Apple ROM is in this repository.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Big-endian, and it is the board's job to say so
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A 68000 reads a word high byte first, and rsemu carries byte order per
# *region* rather than per initiator, so nothing checks that a machine put a
# 68000 on a big-endian map: get it wrong and every word is swapped and the
# reset vector points somewhere absurd. `m68k-mini.machine` has the long
# version. The space says so, and every `map` below either repeats it or names
# a region that declares it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The address map
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   $00 0000 - $3F FFFF   memory - or the ROM while the overlay is up
#   $40 0000 - $4F FFFF   ROM, repeating every 128 KiB
#   $58 0000 - $5F FFFF   SCSI (an NCR 5380) - not modelled; floats
#   $60 0000 - $7F FFFF   memory while the overlay is up; nothing afterwards
#   $80 0000 - $9F FFFF   the SCC, read
#   $A0 0000 - $BF FFFF   the SCC, written
#   $C0 0000 - $DF FFFF   the IWM
#   $E8 0000 - $EF FFFF   the VIA
#
# Two things about that map are worth saying out loud.
#
# **The ROM is at $400000 and also at zero.** The processor's first two
# fetches are the two longwords at $000000 and $000004, and the second of them
# is a program counter pointing into $40xxxx - so the machine only starts
# because the overlay puts the ROM under the vector table, and only keeps going
# because the same ROM answers at its own address as well.
#
# **Main memory does not repeat, and the ROM does.** A 128 KiB ROM answers all
# through the megabyte its select decodes, because the socket carries no
# address line above its own. Memory is the opposite: it answers where it is
# and the rest of its 4 MiB window floats. That is the mechanism the ROM sizes
# memory by - it writes at the top of each candidate size and reads it back -
# and it is not a guess: a decoder that folded the address made this ROM write
# `MemTop = $00400000` on a board with a megabyte in it, put its screen buffer
# where there was no memory, and loop in its memory test for ever.
# `src/dev/mac/glue.rs` has the detail.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is deliberately not mapped, and what an access to it does
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# $50 0000 - $57 FFFF   nothing
# $58 0000 - $5F FFFF   the SCSI controller, which this build has no model of
# $F0 0000 - $FF FFFF   the phase-read space
#
# An access to any of them **completes and floats**: the space's `unassigned`
# policy is `open-bus`. A Macintosh Plus has no bus-error timeout on these
# ranges - /DTACK is generated by the address decoder for everything the board
# claims and the processor simply reads whatever the bus is holding - and the
# MC68000 user's manual (section 5.4) leaves /BERR to external circuitry a
# board may omit. A `fault` policy here turns the ROM's first probe of a chip
# this build does not have into a bus error, and the startup code has nowhere
# to take it.

machine "mac-plus" {
  # -- how much memory -------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # A Plus shipped with 1 MiB in four SIMM sockets and took 2 MiB or 4 MiB.
  # Whatever this is, it answers only where it is, the rest of the 4 MiB window
  # floats, and the video circuit hangs the screen buffer below the *top of
  # what is fitted* - so `-p ram=4M` moves the screen as a real expansion
  # does, and the ROM finds the size for itself.
  param ram = 1M

  # How many mechanisms are on the disk cable: 1 is a Plus with its internal
  # drive alone, 2 adds the external one at the back.
  param drives = 1

  # -- the crystal -----------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # One oscillator: 15.6672 MHz, the dot clock. The processor is half of it,
  # 7.8336 MHz, and the VIA is the 68000's E clock, a tenth of the processor.
  # Every ratio on this board is therefore exact (ROADMAP.md 4.2), which is
  # what lets the video circuit's 60.15 Hz interrupt be dot-exact against the
  # processor rather than approximately right.
  param master = 15667200
  osc   clk    = master Hz

  # -- the bus ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # 24 bits. A 68000 has 23 address pins and A0 is implied by the two data
  # strobes, so the chip drives a 16-megabyte space and the top byte of a
  # 32-bit address never leaves the package - which is exactly why the VIA is
  # at $EFE1FE and not somewhere a 32-bit machine would put it. Empty
  # addresses float rather than fault: see the block above the machine.
  space mem { width = 24, endian = "big", unassigned = open-bus }

  # -- the processor ---------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `model` defaults to 68000, which is what a Plus has.
  object cpu "cpu.m68k" {
    clock  = clk / 2
    space  = mem
    engine = "interp"
  }

  # -- memory ----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `image` names a media slot: whoever runs the machine binds the bytes
  # (`--media macrom=Mac-Plus.ROM`), and nothing below host/ opens a file. A
  # Plus ROM is 128 KiB. Some ROM files carry trailing bytes that are not part
  # of the ROM at all - the one this board was developed against has 7,504 of
  # them - so trim the image to 128 KiB first; the socket has nowhere to put
  # the rest (`docs/platforms/mac-plus.md`).
  object dram "ram" { size = ram }
  object rom  "rom" { size = 128K, image = "macrom" }

  # -- the address decoder ---------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/glue.rs`. It names its two sources as *objects* rather
  # than addresses, because RAM's only address on this machine is zero and so
  # is the decoder's own: there would be nothing to look up.
  object glue "mac.glue" { rom = rom, ram = dram }

  # -- the VIA ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/via.rs`. On the E clock, one tenth of the processor -
  # which is `clk / 20`, written against the crystal like every other clock
  # here. Its sixteen registers are 512 bytes apart because the board puts the
  # chip's register selects on A9-A12, and the block repeats every 8 KiB
  # through the half-megabyte the chip select decodes.
  object via "mac.via" { clock = clk / 20 }

  # -- the video circuit -----------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/video.rs`. It has no registers and no memory: it reads
  # main memory in step with the beam, which is why it names `dram` and why
  # nothing maps it. Its clock is the dot clock, one tick a pixel.
  object video "mac.video" { clock = clk, ram = dram }

  # -- the disk controller ---------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/iwm.rs`. Its sixteen soft switches are 512 bytes apart on
  # the same A9-A12 decode as the VIA's registers, and the block repeats every
  # 8 KiB through the two megabytes its select covers. `$DFE1FF` is the
  # published base and it is an *odd* address: the IWM sits on the low byte
  # lane where the VIA sits on the high one.
  #
  # One mechanism, the internal drive. `-p drives=2` adds the external one.
  object iwm "mac.iwm" { drives = drives }

  # -- the SCC ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # See `src/dev/mac/scc.rs`. Two windows, because the board has no read/write
  # pin for the chip and decodes the direction from the address instead.
  object scc "mac.scc" { }

  # -- the memory map --------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # RAM is *not* mapped directly. It is reached through `glue.low`, which
  # decides per access whether the ROM or the RAM answers - one decoder in
  # front of both, rather than two mappings that would have to be swapped from
  # inside the VIA write that clears the overlay.
  map mem 0x000000 size 0x400000 = glue.low  { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0x600000 size 0x200000 = glue.high { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0x400000 size 0x100000 = mirror(rom) { endian = "big" }

  # Only A1 and A2 reach the SCC, so each window repeats every eight bytes
  # through the two megabytes its select covers. $9FFFF8 is channel B's
  # control address in the read window and $BFFFF9 is its write address, and
  # both land on offset 0 of a copy.
  map mem 0x800000 size 0x200000 = mirror(scc.read)  { endian = "big" }
  map mem 0xA00000 size 0x200000 = mirror(scc.write) { endian = "big" }

  # The VIA's four register selects are on A9-A12 and nothing above them, so
  # its 8 KiB block repeats sixty-four times through $E80000-$EFFFFF. $EFE1FE,
  # the address every published Macintosh equate is written against, is
  # register 0 of the last copy, the one at $EFE000; $EFFFFE is register 15 of
  # it, which is `vBufA`.
  map mem 0xE80000 size 0x80000 = mirror(via) { endian = "big" }

  # The same decode for the IWM, over the two megabytes its own select covers.
  # $DFE1FF is switch 0 of the copy at $DFE000.
  map mem 0xC00000 size 0x200000 = mirror(iwm) { endian = "big" }

  # -- wiring ----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # "PA4  ROMOVERLAY" (the Guide's VIA port-assignment table). Active high, and
  # out of reset the VIA's port A is all inputs, so the pin shows the pull-up
  # and the ROM is at zero before any code has run. Software clears the overlay
  # by making PA4 an output and writing a zero, once it has sized memory.
  wire via.pa4 -> glue.overlay { pull = "up" }

  # Interrupts. A Macintosh has no priority encoder: the VIA's /IRQ goes
  # straight to IPL0 and the SCC's /INT straight to IPL1, so the VIA alone is
  # level 1, the SCC alone is level 2, and both at once really is level 3.
  # That is the hardware, not a simplification. (IPL2 is the programmer's
  # switch, which is a button this board does not have.)
  wire via.irq -> cpu.ipl0
  wire scc.irq -> cpu.ipl1

  # The video circuit's two blanking signals. Vertical blanking is the VIA's
  # CA1 and is the 60.15 Hz interrupt the whole Macintosh tick chain hangs off;
  # `H4` is PB6, which software polls rather than taking an interrupt from.
  #
  # Both nets carry a `pull`, and that is not decoration. The realize sweep
  # (ROADMAP.md 4.3) refreshes a *resolved* net so every sink learns its idle
  # level; a per-sink net has no level of its own, so a driver whose output
  # already matches a fresh source's default announces nothing and the VIA
  # comes up holding the pull-up its pin has with nothing wired to it. The
  # first blanking interval then looks like an edge that has already happened.
  # `H4` really is a pulled-up line; vertical blanking idles low.
  wire video.vblank -> via.ca1 { pull = "down" }
  wire video.hblank -> via.pb6 { pull = "up" }

  # "PA6  PAGE2": which of the two screen buffers the beam reads. Low selects
  # the alternate one.
  wire via.pa6 -> video.page2 { pull = "up" }

  # "PA5  SEL": the fourth address bit of the disk drive's own register file.
  # It lives on the VIA rather than on the IWM because the IWM has only three
  # `CA` lines to spend on it, and the mechanism needs four.
  wire via.pa5 -> iwm.sel { pull = "up" }
}
